00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:09.500
Hi welcome to today's NCBI minute. I will
be talking about author disambiguation and

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:14.360
the strategies and tools in PubMed that will
help you create a comprehensive and precise

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:21.450
list of publications for an author, whether
the author is you or someone else.

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:28.220
Let me get to the agenda slide, we will talk
about the advantage of registering for an

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:35.070
ORCID ID, which is a unique author identifier
you can submit with your publications to PubMed

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:41.659
or include in your bibliography or CV. Beyond
that we will talk about tools that will help

00:01:41.659 --> 00:01:46.299
you manage your own bibliography. This gives
you the control to tweak and customize your

00:01:46.299 --> 00:01:51.420
citation list, then decide if you want to
share it or assign a delegate to assist with

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:52.890
managing it.

00:01:52.890 --> 00:02:00.500
When I talk about ambiguation or disambiguation
in PubMed, there are two flavors of ambiguous

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:06.110
author names. There is the case, and we see
this a lot in PubMed, where there is a single

00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:13.060
author published under many names, if they
had changed their surname for any reason,

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:21.730
got married, this adds a layer of complexity.
Always search with the last name and initials.

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:29.750
The three names you see here. Smith, Smith
Jones, and Jones are three unique last names

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:36.400
in PubMed. For complete retrieval for this
author's citations you should use the OR to

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:42.510
combine the various names that the author
has published with. The second flavor of ambiguity

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:49.650
comes from a single name that could belong
to many different authors in PubMed. The best

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:54.340
way to search is for last name and initials.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:03:01.820
In this case I am searching for young m, I'm
getting more than 4000 results. The fastest

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:07.191
way to narrow this down is to add a middle
initial if I know it. Another way is to add

00:03:07.191 --> 00:03:12.940
topics. Since I am looking for the Nobel Prize
winning scientist who studied circadian clocks,

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:15.129
I could add that term here.

00:03:15.129 --> 00:03:22.480
But since I know the middle name, I will add
a w, this will target my search.

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:26.819
Another way to confirm this is the author
you are looking for, beyond the middle initial

00:03:26.819 --> 00:03:33.140
or narrowing your subject terms, is looking
at the affiliation data. I have additional

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:39.030
evidence that this is that M, W, young I am
looking for. He's affiliated with the lab

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:45.750
at the Rockefeller Univerisity. We have included
the affiliation data for all authors since

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:54.110
October 2013. In December 2014 we have accepted
multiple affiliations for authors. These data

00:03:54.110 --> 00:04:00.049
really helps us identify whether or not we
found the author that we are actually looking

00:04:00.049 --> 00:04:01.500
for.

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:08.370
If I click on the author name from the abstract
view, the 138 citations in my result list

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:14.490
will be sorted by computed author, which weighs
important citation information like subject

00:04:14.490 --> 00:04:21.260
terms, co-authors and affiliations, and clusters
them in order that they are most likely from

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:26.940
the same person. We think this algorithm is
very successful since we see users find what

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:32.220
they are looking for on the first page or
two of the results when they sort this way.

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:37.840
I am not published in PubMed, so I cannot
use my own name, but if you are an author,

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:42.840
searching for your own citations in PubMed
it would be very easy to choose a starting

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:47.310
point, view the citation in abstract view,
and to click on your name.

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:53.780
You will see this sort by author label above
your results. Of course we know some of these

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:58.830
retrieval methods may include some that are
not yours or leave out some that are especially

00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:05.240
if you changed your name. We accept unique
identifiers. The publisher can submit this

00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:13.460
number with the citation data. Publishers
can update existing records and add identifiers

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:20.150
retrospectively. This unique author identifier
is part of that Sort by author algorithm we

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:27.889
just saw. In addition to co-authors, affiliation
data, subject terms, and your name, we are

00:05:27.889 --> 00:05:34.199
also adding in the ORCID ID, which helps with
sort by author.

00:05:34.199 --> 00:05:42.430
ORCID , if you are not familiar stands for
Open Researcher and Contributor Identification.

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:48.050
It is a persistent identifier and an industry
standard that can be applied not just to scholarly

00:05:48.050 --> 00:05:54.970
publishing but other areas of research and
sharing, such as data, blogs, software codes,

00:05:54.970 --> 00:06:03.550
and much more. It has become a broadly accepted
mechanism for identifying ownership of research.

00:06:03.550 --> 00:06:10.080
It's continued success depends on the collaboration
between researchers, publishers, universities,

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:16.030
granting agencies and other stakeholders in
scholarly communications. I think that NLM

00:06:16.030 --> 00:06:21.900
falls solidly in the last category as a stakeholder.
We are encouraging all researchers to register

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:28.169
for ORCID ID that they submit with there publications
and other output.

00:06:28.169 --> 00:06:34.240
We've integrated ORCID with our SciENcv platform
which aids in the creation of the NIH Biosketch

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:40.960
used in the grant management process. The
advantage of ORCID ID, is that it is a persistent

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:48.039
identifier unlike a name that could change
over time. You can search in PubMed just by

00:06:48.039 --> 00:06:54.900
entering the ORCID ID, you do not need to
add a field tag to it, PubMed is really good

00:06:54.900 --> 00:07:02.150
sensing that you are searching for the ORCID
ID based on the pattern of that number.

00:07:02.150 --> 00:07:07.180
In addition to the author sort, additional
affiliation information and the inclusion

00:07:07.180 --> 00:07:12.611
of ORCID ID, my Bib, My Bibliography is a
powerful tool that lets you save and share

00:07:12.611 --> 00:07:21.780
your citations. You can sign in to My NCBI
with your existing Google, NIH or ERA Commons

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:29.810
account. If you click on the link there, See
more third-party sign in options, this will

00:07:29.810 --> 00:07:33.860
include many universities and you can use
your existing credentials to register for

00:07:33.860 --> 00:07:41.009
My NCBI as well. Or create an independent
account just for NCBI. The process is intuitive,

00:07:41.009 --> 00:07:45.500
but you can always contact the support center
if you run into trouble.

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:50.830
This is the My NCBI homepage just to set some
context if you have never looked at this before.

00:07:50.830 --> 00:07:58.470
You can customize this page, reorganize these
portals on the page, think of these as Windows

00:07:58.470 --> 00:08:02.750
into an existing NCBI resource.

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:06.350
You can create and share your bibliography,
even if you are not applying for grants or

00:08:06.350 --> 00:08:12.759
managing grant reporting, however much of
the features and design of My Bib came in

00:08:12.759 --> 00:08:18.580
parallel with the NIH Public Access compliance
needs. It helps with reporting the progress

00:08:18.580 --> 00:08:24.420
of the scientific research. There are four
ways to build your bibliography, I will go

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:26.580
over them now.

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:32.380
From within PubMed the process is simple,
you execute an author search, select some

00:08:32.380 --> 00:08:38.200
citations, and then use the send to menu to
send citations to your bibliography.

00:08:38.200 --> 00:08:43.910
Click on save. You will get an explicit confirmation
and you will be returned to PubMed, because

00:08:43.910 --> 00:08:47.810
that is where you started, but there is a
link at the top and the bottom that says Edit

00:08:47.810 --> 00:08:54.430
your bibliography, this will take you back
to your My Bib page to edit your bibliography.

00:08:54.430 --> 00:08:59.660
If you want to add citations while you are
in your My NCBI account, click on My NCBI

00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:05.730
to get started. This link appears at the top
of all PubMed pages. Then click on Manage

00:09:05.730 --> 00:09:08.850
my bibliography there in the My Bib portal.

00:09:08.850 --> 00:09:14.310
You will see the three options next to your
bibliography. If you do not have any journal

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:19.250
articles they will not appear, I have been
adding citations that are not my own to my

00:09:19.250 --> 00:09:24.310
bibliography, you can see this is populated
and then I have the three options for adding

00:09:24.310 --> 00:09:33.660
citations. If you click on Add from PubMed,
you will see a search box, you can execute

00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:35.660
your search from within My Bib.

00:09:35.660 --> 00:09:42.680
On this screen you can select citations to
add and it shows citations that are already

00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:49.410
part of your bibliographies. If you see anything
with a greyed out checkmark and grey text,

00:09:49.410 --> 00:09:56.300
you can tell that those are already in your
bibliography and not an option for adding.

00:09:56.300 --> 00:10:01.779
To get to this screen, again, we're starting
by clicking My NCBI, manage my bibliography,

00:10:01.779 --> 00:10:08.040
then from the menu you saw before, select,
Add manually, to get this form you can fill

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:13.700
in. There's a picklist at the top, we have
selected Books and chapters, which is not

00:10:13.700 --> 00:10:21.110
something that traditionally appears, so you
would not necessarily have a citation to import.

00:10:21.110 --> 00:10:27.890
The other options on the list include Journal
articles that do not appear in PubMed, meeting

00:10:27.890 --> 00:10:35.260
abstracts, presentations, patents, data sets
or database, software and I think a couple

00:10:35.260 --> 00:10:41.610
others. If you select upload a file. You will
use your browser's file management system

00:10:41.610 --> 00:10:47.519
to upload any Medline or bibliography citation
files.

00:10:47.519 --> 00:10:55.540
You can see the two options available. A medline
file. Then an mbib, a bibliography citation

00:10:55.540 --> 00:10:56.540
format.

00:10:56.540 --> 00:11:01.529
Now you have a bibliography and you can change
the settings, make Public or Private, you

00:11:01.529 --> 00:11:10.721
can share the URL if the collection is public,
you can edit settings, look at letter B, I

00:11:10.721 --> 00:11:15.149
will go into that part in more detail.

00:11:15.149 --> 00:11:20.180
Where you see the letter C, you can modify
your display settings. You can see right now

00:11:20.180 --> 00:11:25.110
it says List view, sort by date, group by
citation type. There are other options you

00:11:25.110 --> 00:11:27.240
can explore there.

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:31.360
You can look at these actions that are available,
they are all very intuitive. You can move

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:37.019
citations from your bibliography to another
collection. A bibliography is just a special

00:11:37.019 --> 00:11:43.959
collection within My NCBI named for you, it
behaves like any other collection you have

00:11:43.959 --> 00:11:51.079
created. You can move citations, delete citations,
copy them, and view. You can also suggest.

00:11:51.079 --> 00:11:57.410
This is the one feature that you may not have
anticipated or are familiar with. You can

00:11:57.410 --> 00:12:03.260
actually check next to a citation, you can
suggest for example to a co-author that they

00:12:03.260 --> 00:12:08.510
add it to their bibliography as well. When
you send that suggestion through email they

00:12:08.510 --> 00:12:13.139
have a chance to review it and either accept
or refuse that suggestion.

00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:19.589
I put this letter E here so that you can see
how many links are available there. This is

00:12:19.589 --> 00:12:25.050
a bibliography but there are a lot of links
in the results in PubMed, you can link to

00:12:25.050 --> 00:12:30.920
the PubMed citation if you click on the title,
or if the fulltext is available it will tell

00:12:30.920 --> 00:12:39.140
you. You can link out to the free full text.
You can also find Related citations. I encourage

00:12:39.140 --> 00:12:43.881
you to explore this page. It's very intuitive
but there is extensive online help for my

00:12:43.881 --> 00:12:45.850
bibliography.

00:12:45.850 --> 00:12:53.260
I will go ahead and look at what we see if
we click on Edit settings. Because my bibliography

00:12:53.260 --> 00:13:04.700
is public, I will see a direct URL and the
HTML that I can add to webpages and blogs.

00:13:04.700 --> 00:13:10.279
If my bibliography was set to private, those
options would not be available for sharing,

00:13:10.279 --> 00:13:15.790
but that is how you share your bibliography,
either email your URL to people or they have

00:13:15.790 --> 00:13:19.710
the HTML to embed in a webpage or blog.

00:13:19.710 --> 00:13:25.579
You can assign delegates as well. You do this
through the Account Settings page. You can

00:13:25.579 --> 00:13:31.030
see that you can link at the bottom of your
My Bibliography settings, but this is the

00:13:31.030 --> 00:13:35.730
same if I clicked on my username at the top
of the page, that will take me to my settings

00:13:35.730 --> 00:13:38.519
and I can assign a delegate.

00:13:38.519 --> 00:13:46.421
Now if I go to My NCBI settings, you can see
delegates option. I can Add a delegate. It

00:13:46.421 --> 00:13:52.620
just is a matter of adding the person's email
and then selecting the service I want them

00:13:52.620 --> 00:14:00.820
to be a delegate for, whether just My Bibliography,
or My bib and SciENcv, which is a tool we

00:14:00.820 --> 00:14:08.449
use to take all of this bibliography information,
helping in the processing and exporting of

00:14:08.449 --> 00:14:15.889
that data into an NIH Biosketch or other types
of Biosketches, format, that are available.

00:14:15.889 --> 00:14:22.830
Today I talked about ORCID IDs and My bibliographt
in the context of author disambiguation, and

00:14:22.830 --> 00:14:26.970
tools for identifying, collecting and sharing
your citations.

00:14:26.970 --> 00:14:33.029
These tools are also a very powerful part
of the grant application and compliance process.

00:14:33.029 --> 00:14:37.500
Very quickly I want to show that My Bib has
even more features if you have logged in with

00:14:37.500 --> 00:14:41.769
your ERA Commons account or have linked the
two accounts.

00:14:41.769 --> 00:14:47.690
This helps track citations and manage the
compliance progress. If you are not familiar

00:14:47.690 --> 00:14:53.560
ERA Commons, it is an online interface where
grant applicants, grantees and federal staff

00:14:53.560 --> 00:15:00.450
at NIH and granting agencies can access and
share information related to research grants.

00:15:00.450 --> 00:15:06.320
If you look at this page you can see there
are actually different symbols whether exclamtion

00:15:06.320 --> 00:15:13.910
points or yellow that will tell you the status
of these citations in terms of the funding

00:15:13.910 --> 00:15:15.430
process.

00:15:15.430 --> 00:15:21.459
So today in this NCBI Minute I mentioned a
